# Python Port Plan — Step 11: A Terminal UI

## Goal

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/11_tui` to `week1_baseline/python/11_tui`. Ruby adds
a full terminal UI on top of step 10's plain REPL, built on the `charm` gem
(Ruby bindings to Go's bubbletea + lipgloss + bubbles). `Boukensha.repl` gains
a `tui:` keyword (default `true`); `--no-tui` on the CLI falls back to the
plain REPL from step 10. `Repl` is refactored so its I/O is driven through
callbacks (`on_output`, `handle_command`, `run_turn`) instead of hard-coded
`puts`/`gets`, so a `Tui` class (or any other front-end) can wrap it. Version
bumps to `0.11.0`.

There is no direct Python equivalent of the `charm`/bubbletea stack, so this
is a genuine reimplementation rather than a mechanical port for the UI layer
itself — confirmed with the user:

- **TUI library: Textual.** Chosen over `prompt_toolkit`/`urwid` because its
  built-in widgets map directly onto the four zones described below (`RichLog`
  for the scrollable conversation viewport, `Input` for the textarea,
  reactive attributes + `set_interval` for the live progress/status lines),
  it has a built-in key-binding system, and CSS-based styling as the lipgloss
  analog — the closest architectural match to bubbletea's Model/Update/View
  loop.
- **Esc-interrupt: cooperative cancellation flag.** Ruby's Esc handler calls
  `@turn_thread.raise(Interrupt)` — Ruby can deliver an async exception into a
  running thread at (almost) any point, including mid blocking I/O. Python
  threads have no safe equivalent (the `ctypes.pythonapi.PyThreadState_SetAsyncExc`
  trick exists but can corrupt state mid-syscall and is not appropriate for
  teaching code). Instead: `Agent` gets an optional `cancel_event:
  threading.Event | None` constructor param, checked once per loop iteration
  (after the current model/tool round-trip completes, before starting the
  next one); if set, it raises a new `TurnCancelled` exception that unwinds
  cleanly up through `Repl.run_turn`. This means a turn can only be
  interrupted **between** iterations, not mid-API-call or mid-tool-call — a
  real, documented behavior difference from Ruby, not a bug.

