## The Tui class — behavior breakdown (for the Textual translation)

Ruby's `Tui` (bubbletea `Model`) has:

- **State**: `@repl`, `@context`, `@events` (a `Queue`), `@conversation`
  (array of strings), `@dirty` flag, `@turn_count`, session-level token
  totals, `@turn_thread`, a `@live` hash (spinner/elapsed/iteration/action/
  tool-call-count/turn-token-counts), `@width`/`@height`, a `Bubbles::Viewport`,
  a `Bubbles::TextArea`.
- **`start`**: seeds `@conversation` with `repl.banner`, registers
  `repl.on_output { |str| @conversation << str; @dirty = true }` and
  `repl.logger.subscribe { |event| @events << event }`, focuses the textarea,
  then runs the bubbletea event loop (`alt_screen: true`, 50ms input poll,
  30fps).
- **`init`**: returns self plus a recurring `TickMsg` command (every 60ms).
- **`update(msg)`**: on `WindowSizeMessage` resizes the viewport; on `TickMsg`
  drains the event queue and advances the spinner/elapsed time, then
  reschedules the next tick; on `KeyMessage` dispatches to `handle_key`,
  quitting the program if it returns `:quit`.
- **`view`**: re-syncs the viewport content from `@conversation` if dirty,
  then joins `[viewport, progress line, input line, status line]` with `\n`.
- **`handle_key`**: `ctrl+c`/`ctrl+d` → quit; `esc` → raise `Interrupt` into
  `@turn_thread` if alive; `ctrl+l` → `repl.handle_command("/clear")` +
  reset turn count; `pgup`/`pgdown` → scroll viewport; `enter` →
  `submit_input`; anything else → forward the keystroke to the textarea.
- **`submit_input`**: strips/ignores blank input; if it starts with `/`,
  routes through `repl.handle_command`, quitting or resetting turn count as
  needed; otherwise appends `"> #{input}"` to the conversation and calls
  `launch_turn`.
- **`launch_turn`**: resets `@live`, spawns a `Thread` that calls
  `repl.run_turn(input)`, pushing a `turn_interrupted`/`turn_error`/
  `turn_complete` event onto `@events` in `rescue`/`ensure`.
- **`drain_events`/`handle_event`**: non-blocking-drains the queue each tick;
  updates `@live`'s iteration/action/tool-call-count/token counts based on
  `event[:phase]` (`"iteration"`, `"tool_call"`, `"tool_result"`,
  `"response"`, `"turn_complete"`, `"turn_interrupted"`, `"turn_error"`).
- **Rendering helpers**: `render_progress` (spinner + action + iteration/max
  + elapsed + token counts + tool-call count when active, else `[ready]` +
  context tokens + turn count), `render_input` (green bold prompt + textarea),
  `render_status` (version · model · context tokens · tool count · clock,
  full-width bar), `lip` (lipgloss style builder for fg/bg/bold), `fmt_tokens`
  (`n >= 1000` → `"{n/1000:.1f}k"`, else plain int).

### Textual translation shape

- `Boukensha::Tui` → `boukensha.tui.BoukenshaApp(textual.app.App)`.
- Four zones → three-widget vertical layout: a `RichLog` (or `Static` inside
  a `VerticalScroll`) for the conversation viewport, a `Static` for the
  progress line, a Textual `Input` for the entry box, and a `Static` (or
  Textual's built-in status bar convention) for the status line. Bubbles'
  `Viewport`'s `goto_bottom` maps to `RichLog.scroll_end`/auto-scroll.
  `pgup`/`pgdown` map to `RichLog.scroll_page_up`/`scroll_page_down` (or a
  key binding calling the same).
- `TickMsg` / 60ms timer → `self.set_interval(TICK_MS / 1000, self._tick)`
  started in `on_mount`.
- The bubbletea `init`/`update`/`view` triple collapses into Textual's
  `on_mount` (subscribe to `repl.logger`, seed conversation, start timer),
  reactive attributes + `watch_*` methods (re-render on change instead of a
  manual `@dirty` flag — Textual's reactive system is the built-in analog),
  and `compose`/widget `.update(...)` calls instead of a single `view` string.
- **Threading bridge**: `repl.logger.subscribe(...)` and `repl.on_output(...)`
  callbacks fire on the *worker thread* running the agent turn (see below),
  but Textual widgets must only be mutated from the app's own thread/event
  loop. Both callbacks must marshal onto the main thread via
  `self.call_from_thread(...)` — this is the direct Python analog of the
  Ruby version's `Queue`-based `@events`/`drain_events` polling pattern
  (Ruby needs the queue because bubbletea's tick loop polls it manually;
  Textual's `call_from_thread` achieves the same cross-thread safety more
  directly, so the manual queue/drain-on-tick machinery is not needed in the
  Python port — document this as an intentional simplification enabled by
  the target framework, not a functional gap).
- **`launch_turn`** → a method decorated `@work(thread=True, exclusive=True)`
  (Textual's built-in background-worker API) instead of a raw
  `threading.Thread` — this is the natural Textual idiom for "run blocking
  code without freezing the UI" and replaces Ruby's manual `Thread.new` +
  `rescue`/`ensure` bookkeeping. Catch `TurnCancelled` distinctly (→
  `turn_interrupted`, mirroring Ruby's `rescue Interrupt`) from any other
  `Exception` (→ `turn_error`).
- **`esc` key** → calls `self.repl.interrupt()` (see Design decisions), a new
  method that sets the shared `cancel_event`, instead of Ruby's
  `@turn_thread.raise(Interrupt)`.
- **`event[:phase] || event["phase"]`** — Ruby's dual read exists because
  in-process Ruby hashes built from keyword args use symbol keys; the
  `event["phase"]` fallback is dead code in practice. Python's `Logger`
  already emits string-only-keyed dicts (established since step 6), so
  `handle_event` just does `event["phase"]` — no dual-read needed, same
  simplification already applied elsewhere in this port.
- `ANSI_COLORS` / `lip()` → Textual CSS classes or inline `Style` objects
  (Textual has its own richer color system; reuse the same four named
  colors — cyan, bright_black, green, white — as CSS rules in a companion
  stylesheet or inline styles, whichever keeps `tui.py` self-contained
  without a separate `.css` file, matching this codebase's one-file-per-
  concern style so far).

## Design decisions (confirmed with user)

1. **Textual** as the TUI framework (see Goal section for rationale).
2. **Cooperative cancellation** via `Agent(cancel_event=...)` +
   `TurnCancelled` exception, checked once per agent-loop iteration boundary,
   instead of Ruby's `Thread#raise(Interrupt)`. `Repl` gains a
   `self._cancel_event = threading.Event()` created once in `__init__`, a
   new public `interrupt()` method (`self._cancel_event.set()`), and clears
   the event at the top of each `run_turn` call before constructing the
   `Agent`. This keeps `Repl`'s other public methods' signatures identical
   to Ruby's (`on_output`, `handle_command`, `run_turn`).

## Porting notes / gotchas to handle deliberately

1. **`/quiet`/`/loud` and `Boukensha.quiet!`/`loud!`/`quiet?` are dropped
   entirely in Ruby step 11** — not a README-drift issue, a real code change
   (confirmed via diff: both removed from `repl.rb` and `boukensha.rb`, and
   nothing replaces them). Per established precedent (reproduce Ruby's
   reversions faithfully, e.g. step 9's config-resolution reversion, step
   10's banner reversion), the Python port must remove:
   - The `/quiet`/`/loud` branches, and their `HELP`/banner lines, from
     `repl.py`.
   - `enable_quiet`, `disable_quiet`, `is_quiet` from `_state.py` and their
     re-exports from `__init__.py.__all__` — grep confirms these are used
     *only* by `repl.py`'s two removed branches, so removing them is a clean
     deletion with no dangling references elsewhere in `mud_session.py`,
     `tools/mud.py`, etc. (those files' unrelated `read_until_quiet` /
     `quiet_seconds` names are a naming coincidence, not related to this
     state).
2. **The `MUD_NAME`/`MUD_HOST`/`MUD_PORT`/`MUD_PASSWORD` legacy env-var
   override block in `boukensha_loader.rb`'s `load_and_start_repl` was
   already present in Ruby step 10**, but was missed when
   `python/10_standard_tool_library/lib/boukensha_loader.py` was ported (its
   `main()` calls `module.repl()` with no options at all). This plan does
   not go back and patch step 10; it ports the full logic — env override
   block *and* the new `--no-tui` handling — into step 11's
   `boukensha_loader.py.main()`, since step 11's Ruby source depends on both
   pieces together and step 11 is otherwise a faithful full port. `main()`
   becomes:
   ```python
   def main() -> None:
       ...
       no_tui = "--no-tui" in sys.argv
       if no_tui:
           sys.argv.remove("--no-tui")

       repl_opts: dict[str, Any] = {"tui": not no_tui}

       if os.environ.get("MUD_NAME"):
           repl_opts["working_dir"] = False
           repl_opts["mud"] = {
               "host": os.environ.get("MUD_HOST", "localhost"),
               "port": int(os.environ.get("MUD_PORT", "4000")),
               "name": os.environ["MUD_NAME"],
               "password": os.environ.get("MUD_PASSWORD") or sys.exit(
                   "boukensha: MUD_NAME is set but MUD_PASSWORD is missing."
               ),
           }

       module.repl(**repl_opts)
   ```
3. **`if tui && defined?(Tui)` guard.** In Ruby this is defensive style —
   `boukensha.rb` unconditionally `require_relative "boukensha/tui"`s at the
   bottom of the file, so `defined?(Tui)` is always true in practice. Python
   doesn't need an equivalent guard: `tui.py` is unconditionally importable
   once `textual` is installed (a hard `pyproject.toml` dependency, not
   optional), so `run_dsl.py`'s `repl()` just does
   `if tui: BoukenshaApp(repl_instance).run() else: repl_instance.start()`.
4. **`Agent`'s new `cancel_event` param is Python-only** — Ruby's
   `agent.rb` is byte-identical to step 10 and has no cancellation concept at
   all (Ruby achieves interruption entirely via `Thread#raise` from outside
   the loop). Document this clearly in the Python README as an addition
   needed to make Esc-interrupt safe under Python's threading model, not as
   a 1:1 translation of any Ruby code.
5. **Slash-command HELP text** in `repl.py`'s `HELP` constant and `_banner()`
   method must drop the `/quiet or /loud   toggle logging` lines to match
   Ruby's step-11 `repl.rb` exactly (see Porting note 1).
6. **`patches/bubbletea/`** — do not attempt to port the C-extension patch or
   the `ntcharts` conflict workaround; note in the README that Textual has no
   native-extension burst-read bug or competing-Go-runtime issue to work
   around, so this entire directory has no Python counterpart.
7. **Version bump**: `version.py` → `"0.11.0"`, `pyproject.toml`'s
   `[project].version` → `"0.11.0"`.
8. **`examples/example.py`** carries over unchanged from step 10 (still the
   MUD demo, `working_dir=False`) — copy verbatim, no TUI-specific example
   needed since the TUI is only reachable via the `boukensha` executable, per
   the Ruby README's own statement.
9. **New `pyproject.toml` dependency**: add `"textual"` alongside
   `python-dotenv`, `PyYAML`, `requests`.

