## Acceptance criteria

- `pip install -e .` succeeds with `textual` installed; `import boukensha`
  exposes `VERSION == "0.11.0"`, `run`, `repl`, `Tui`/`BoukenshaApp`, and no
  longer exposes `enable_quiet`/`disable_quiet`/`is_quiet`.
- Launching `boukensha` (or the step's bin wrapper) opens the Textual TUI by
  default; `boukensha --no-tui` falls back to the plain `Repl.start()` loop
  from step 10 with `/quiet`/`/loud` no longer present in its `/help` output.
- The TUI's four zones render and update live during a real agent turn
  (conversation viewport, progress line with spinner/iteration/token counts,
  input box, status line), verified interactively since a full-screen TUI
  cannot be meaningfully exercised through scripted/offline tests alone.
- `Esc` during a running turn sets the cancellation flag and the turn stops
  at the next iteration boundary, distinctly reported as `[interrupted]`
  rather than `[error] ...`.
- `Ctrl+L` clears conversation history and resets the turn counter; `PgUp`/
  `PgDn` scroll the viewport; `Ctrl+C`/`Ctrl+D` quit cleanly.
- `MUD_NAME`-based legacy env-var override and `settings.yaml`'s `mud:`
  block both still work as the two ways to configure the MUD tools, matching
  Ruby's `boukensha_loader.rb`.
- No behavior invented beyond what the Ruby reference files define, except
  the two explicitly-confirmed, explicitly-documented Python-only additions
  (Textual as the rendering engine, and the cooperative `cancel_event`
  mechanism replacing `Thread#raise`).
